[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer**

*You can start with a plain `geojson` source for the 8 000 trail segments and a hosted vector-basemap (terrain + roads) as a second source.*  
If you notice a noticeable pause when the map first loads, or if the trails are very detailed (many vertices per segment), switch the trails to a small vector-tile set generated with **tippecanoe** (or **Planetiler**) – but you don’t need a tile server just to make the map work.

Below is a step-by-step guide, the “why” behind each decision, and the “what-if” path for later scaling.

---

## 1. Why GeoJSON is fine for ~8 000 trail segments

| Metric | Typical range for 8 000 trail segments | What the spec says |
|--------|----------------------------------------|--------------------|
| **Feature count** | 8 000 (≈ 1.6 × the “sweet-spot” limit of 5 000) | Still well inside the “lag zone” (≤ 50 000) |
| **File size** (after modest simplification) | 2 – 5 MB | Below the 20 MB “lag zone” ceiling; browsers usually parse this in < 2 s on desktop and < 4 s on modern mobiles |
| **Interactivity needed** | Click → name & distance | GeoJSON gives you the full feature properties client-side, no extra server round-trip |

**Result:**  
- One HTTP request for the GeoJSON file.  
- No tile infrastructure to set up.  
- Full access to every property for pop-ups, filtering, editing, etc.

If you later find the initial load is > 3 s on your target devices, you can **simplify** the geometries (reduce vertex count) or **move to vector tiles** – the switch is painless because the rest of the style stays the same.

---

## 2. Recommended source layout (two-source pattern)

| Source name | Type | What it holds | Where it comes from |
|-------------|------|---------------|---------------------|
| `basemap`   | `vector` | Terrain DEM + road & label layers (e.g. OpenMapTiles, MapTiler, OpenFreeMap) | Use a **style URL** or a **TileJSON** vector source |
| `trails`    | `geojson` | Your 8 000 trail segments (LineString or MultiLineString) | Inline object **or** a static file (`/data/trails.geojson`) |

### Example style snippet (JSON)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "basemap": {
      "type": "vector",
      "url": "https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets-v2/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY"
    },
    "trails": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "/data/trails.geojson"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    /* 1️⃣ Terrain & background layers from the basemap style – keep them first */
    /* (you can just use the whole hosted style and then insert your own layers) */

    /* 2️⃣ Insert your trail line layer *before* the first symbol layer so labels stay on top */
    {
      "id": "trails-line",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "trails",
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#ff7f00",
        "line-width": 3
      }
    },

    /* 3️⃣ Optional: a line-halo for better contrast on bright basemaps */
    {
      "id": "trails-line-halo",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "trails",
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#ffffff",
        "line-width": 5,
        "line-opacity": 0.4
      }
    },

    /* 4️⃣ Click-able point layer for pop-ups (if you want a marker at the trail start) */
    {
      "id": "trails-points",
      "type": "circle",
      "source": "trails",
      "filter": ["==", "$type", "Point"],
      "paint": {
        "circle-radius": 5,
        "circle-color": "#ff7f00"
      }
    },

    /* 5️⃣ All remaining basemap layers (roads, labels, terrain hillshade, etc.) */
    /* If you used a full style URL you don’t need to list them – they’re already there */
  ]
}
```

**Key points**

* **Insert your trail layers before the first symbol layer** (road names, place labels).  
  This keeps the labels readable. The `maplibre-source-wiring` skill shows how to find the first symbol layer programmatically.

* **Keep the basemap source separate** – you can swap it for a different provider (satellite, dark theme, offline PMTiles) without touching the trail data.

* **If you need 3-D terrain**, add a `raster-dem` source (e.g. MapTiler DEM tiles) and enable `terrain` in the map options. The basemap already contains hillshade, but you can also add a `hillshade` layer that reads from the DEM.

---

## 3. Adding click interaction (name + distance)

```javascript
map.on('click', 'trails-line', (e) => {
  const props = e.features[0].properties;
  const name = props.name || 'Unnamed trail';
  const distance = props.distance ? `${props.distance} km` : '—';

  new maplibregl.Popup()
    .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
    .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>Length: ${distance}`)
    .addTo(map);
});

// Change cursor on hover
map.on('mouseenter', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
map.on('mouseleave', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
```

Because the source is `geojson`, **all properties are already on the client**, so the popup can be built instantly.

---

## 4. When (and how) to migrate to vector tiles

| Trigger | What to do |
|---------|------------|
| **Initial load > 3 s** on target devices | 1️⃣ Simplify the GeoJSON (e.g. `mapshaper -simplify 10%`). 2️⃣ If still slow, generate a small vector-tile set. |
| **Future growth** (you expect > 20 000 trails or more detailed geometry) | Use **tippecanoe** to turn the GeoJSON into an MBTiles/PMTiles file, then serve it via a static-file host, a CDN, or a lightweight tile server (e.g. `tileserver-gl`, `pmtiles-server`). |
| **Offline / air-gapped deployment** | Package the vector tiles (or even the whole style) into a single PMTiles archive and load it with the `pmtiles` protocol. |

### Minimal tippecanoe command (for a 8 000-feature set)

```bash
tippecanoe \
  --output trails.mbtiles \
  --layer trails \
  --minimum-zoom=5 \
  --maximum-zoom=14 \
  --drop-densest-as-needed \
  --simplify-only-low-zoom \
  trails.geojson
```

* `--minimum-zoom`/`--maximum-zoom` limit the zoom range you actually need (hiking trails rarely need zoom 0-4).  
* `--drop-densest-as-needed` keeps the tile size under the typical 500 KB limit.  
* `--simplify-only-low-zoom` reduces vertex count only for low-zoom tiles, preserving detail when users zoom in.

After you have `trails.mbtiles`, you can either:

* Serve it as **XYZ** tiles (`/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`) with any static-file host, **or**
* Convert it to **PMTiles** (`pmtiles convert trails.mbtiles trails.pmtiles`) and load it with the `pmtiles://` protocol (no extra server needed).

Then change the source definition to:

```json
"trails": {
  "type": "vector",
  "url": "pmtiles://https://example.com/trails.pmtiles"
}
```

All the layer definitions stay the same – only the source type changes.

---

## 5. Full “starter” code (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Hiking Trails + Terrain Basemap</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      "version": 8,
      "sources": {
        "basemap": {
          "type": "vector",
          "url": "https://api.maptiler.com/maps/terrain/style.json?key=YOUR_KEY"
        },
        "trails": {
          "type": "geojson",
          "data": "/data/trails.geojson"
        }
      },
      "layers": [
        // basemap layers are already in the style URL – we just add ours
        {
          "id": "trails-line",
          "type": "line",
          "source": "trails",
          "paint": {
            "line-color": "#ff7f00",
            "line-width": 3
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-122.45, 37.75], // example: San Francisco Bay Area
    zoom: 10,
    pitch: 45,               // optional 3-D terrain view
    antialias: true
  });

  // Enable terrain (requires a raster-dem source inside the basemap style)
  map.on('load', () => {
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'maptiler://terrain-dem?key=YOUR_KEY',
      tileSize: 512,
      maxzoom: 14
    });
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 });
  });

  // Click popup
  map.on('click', 'trails-line', (e) => {
    const f = e.features[0];
    const name = f.properties.name || 'Unnamed trail';
    const dist = f.properties.distance ? `${f.properties.distance} km` : '—';
    new maplibregl.Popup()
      .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
      .setHTML(`<strong>${name}</strong><br/>Length: ${dist}`)
      .addTo(map);
  });

  map.on('mouseenter', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'trails-line', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `YOUR_KEY` with a MapTiler (or other provider) API key, or point the `basemap` URL at any vector-tile style you already have.*

---

## 6. Checklist before you ship

| ✅ | Item |
|----|------|
| **GeoJSON size** – keep the file ≤ 10 MB (preferably ≤ 5 MB). Use `mapshaper` or `ogr2ogr -simplify` if needed. |
| **Properties** – ensure each trail feature has `name` and `distance` (or compute distance on the fly). |
| **Layer order** – insert your trail layers before the first symbol layer of the basemap. |
| **CORS** – host the GeoJSON on a domain that allows cross-origin requests (or serve it from the same origin). |
| **Attribution** – the basemap provider’s attribution must be displayed (MapLibre automatically adds it if the style includes it). |
| **Terrain** – if you want 3-D hills, add a `raster-dem` source and call `map.setTerrain`. |
| **Future-proofing** – keep the GeoJSON file in a version-controlled repo; if you ever cross the “lag zone”, run tippecanoe and swap the source type – no layer changes required. |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Start with a `geojson` source** for the 8 000 trail segments – it’s well within the “lag zone” and gives you instant client-side interactivity.  
2. **Add a hosted vector basemap** (terrain + roads) as a second source.  
3. Insert your

**Short answer:** No – for a store-locator with ~50 points you don’t need a vector-tile server at all. A simple **GeoJSON source** (inline or fetched from a static file) is well within the “sweet-spot” limits ( < 2 MB / < 5 000 features ) and will load instantly on any modern browser.

Below is a quick decision-tree, then a concrete example and a few extra tips you might find handy.

---

## Decision-tree for a 50-store locator

| Question | Answer | Recommended source |
|----------|--------|--------------------|
| **How many features?** | ~50 (tiny) | **GeoJSON** |
| **Do you need per-feature styling that changes at runtime?** | Yes (e.g., highlight a selected store) | GeoJSON gives you full client-side access to properties. |
| **Do you need to support offline / air-gapped use?** | Not in this case | GeoJSON still works; you could bundle the file with the app. |
| **Do you already have a vector-tile basemap?** | Likely (e.g., OpenStreetMap, MapTiler, etc.) | Keep the basemap as a **vector** source and add your stores as a separate **geojson** source. |

Result: **vector basemap + geojson overlay** – the most common pattern for store locators, retail dashboards, and any small-to-medium point dataset.

---

## Minimal working example

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Store Locator – MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map with a hosted vector basemap (any provider that serves MapLibre-compatible vector tiles)
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY', // replace with your basemap URL
    center: [-98.35, 39.5], // continental US centre
    zoom: 4
  });

  // 2️⃣ When the style is ready, add the GeoJSON source for the stores
  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.addSource('stores', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: '/data/stores.geojson'   // could also be an inline object
    });

    // 3️⃣ Add a layer to draw the points
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'store-points',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'stores',
      paint: {
        'circle-radius': 8,
        'circle-color': '#ff6600',
        'circle-stroke-width': 2,
        'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
      }
    });

    // 4️⃣ Optional: add a label layer (place it *before* the first symbol layer so it stays on top of basemap labels)
    const firstSymbolId = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol')?.id;
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'store-labels',
      type: 'symbol',
      source: 'stores',
      layout: {
        'text-field': ['get', 'name'],
        'text-font': ['Open Sans Bold', 'Arial Unicode MS Bold'],
        'text-size': 12,
        'text-offset': [0, 1.2],
        'text-anchor': 'top'
      },
      paint: {
        'text-color': '#000',
        'text-halo-color': '#fff',
        'text-halo-width': 1
      }
    }, firstSymbolId); // insert before first basemap symbol layer
  });

  // 5️⃣ Interaction – show a popup on click
  map.on('click', 'store-points', (e) => {
    const props = e.features[0].properties;
    new maplibregl.Popup()
      .setLngLat(e.lngLat)
      .setHTML(`<strong>${props.name}</strong><br/>${props.address}`)
      .addTo(map);
  });

  // Change cursor on hover
  map.on('mouseenter', 'store-points', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = 'pointer');
  map.on('mouseleave', 'store-points', () => map.getCanvas().style.cursor = '');
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Key points in the code**

* **Basemap** – a hosted vector style (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.). No need to touch the basemap tiles yourself.
* **`stores.geojson`** – a tiny file (a few kilobytes). Example content:

  ```json
  {
    "type": "FeatureCollection",
    "features": [
      {
        "type": "Feature",
        "properties": { "name": "Store A", "address": "123 Main St, Anytown, TX" },
        "geometry": { "type": "Point", "coordinates": [-96.7970, 32.7767] }
      }
      // … 49 more …
    ]
  }
  ```

* **Layer order** – the label layer is inserted *before* the first symbol layer of the basemap so your store names stay readable on top of road/POI labels (see the “Combining source types” section of the skill).

---

## When you *might* consider tiles (not required here)

| Situation | Why tiles could help |
|-----------|----------------------|
| **> 100 k features** or **> 50 MB** GeoJSON | Browser parsing becomes a bottleneck; vector tiles stream only what’s visible. |
| **Zoom-dependent styling** (e.g., show only city-level stores at low zoom, all stores at high zoom) | Tile layers can provide different detail levels per zoom automatically. |
| **Offline kiosk with a single archive** | A single PMTiles/MBTiles file can be shipped and read locally, but you still keep the basemap separate unless you truly need a monolithic package. |
| **Frequent bulk updates** (e.g., thousands of stores added daily) | Regenerating a tiny GeoJSON is cheap; for massive updates you’d need a tile pipeline anyway. |

For a 50-store locator none of the above apply, so stick with GeoJSON.

---

## Extra tips for a smooth store-locator experience

| Tip | Why it matters |
|-----|-----------------|
| **Cluster points** (optional) | Even with 50 points you can enable `cluster: true` on the GeoJSON source to keep the map tidy when zoomed out. |
| **Simplify geometry** | Store locations are points, so no simplification needed. If you ever add polygons (e.g., service areas), keep them low-resolution. |
| **CORS** | Host `stores.geojson` on the same domain or enable CORS headers; otherwise the browser will block the request. |
| **Attribution** | The basemap provider (MapTiler, OpenStreetMap, etc.) will have its own attribution string that you must display. Your store data is yours, but still credit the basemap. |
| **Performance testing** | Open the page on a low-end mobile device; you should see < 200 ms network time and < 50 ms render time for the points. If you ever exceed that, you’ve crossed the “Lag zone” threshold and should revisit the source type. |

---

## TL;DR

* **No vector-tile server needed** for 50 US stores.  
* Use a **GeoJSON source** (inline or static file).  
* Pair it with any **vector basemap** you like, inserting your point and label layers before the basemap’s symbol layers for readability.  
* Only consider tiles if you ever grow to hundreds of thousands of features, need heavy zoom-dependent styling, or must ship a completely offline archive.

Happy mapping! 🚀

Below is a step-by-step recipe for turning a single scanned JPG (or any georeferenced raster image) into a **MapLibre `image` source**, adding it as a raster layer, and exposing a simple opacity toggle.  
The pattern follows the “image source” entry in the **source-type table** of the *maplibre-tile-sources* skill.

---

## 1. Why an `image` source is the right choice

| Situation | Recommended source type | Reason |
|-----------|------------------------|--------|
| One static raster (JPG, PNG, TIFF) that you already know the four corner coordinates | **`image`** | No tiling server needed; MapLibre fetches the single file, projects it onto the map using the supplied corner coordinates, and renders it as a raster layer. |
| Very large image (hundreds of MB, > 10 k px) or you need smooth zoom-in beyond the native resolution | **`raster` tiles** (e.g., PMTiles, XYZ) | The single-image approach will become blurry when you zoom past its native resolution. In that case you would first cut the image into tiles (gdal2tiles, maptiler, tippecanoe-raster, etc.) and serve them as a raster tile source. |

For a typical city-scale scanned map (a few MB, ~2000 px on a side) the `image` source is the simplest and most performant solution.

---

## 2. Prepare the corner coordinates

MapLibre expects the corners in **counter-clockwise order** (top-left → top-right → bottom-right → bottom-left) and each corner as `[lon, lat]` in **WGS-84 (EPSG:4326)**.

```text
// Example – you already have them:
top-left      = [ -122.4525, 37.8044 ]   // lon, lat
top-right     = [ -122.3890, 37.8044 ]
bottom-right  = [ -122.3890, 37.7610 ]
bottom-left   = [ -122.4525, 37.7610 ]
```

If you only have the coordinates in another CRS (e.g., a local state plane), convert them to EPSG:4326 first (proj4, GDAL, QGIS, etc.).

---

## 3. Add the source and layer in JavaScript

```javascript
// 1️⃣  Create the MapLibre map (skip if you already have one)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [-122.42, 37.78],       // initial view
  zoom: 12
});

// 2️⃣  Wait until the style is loaded before adding custom sources
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // ---- IMAGE SOURCE -------------------------------------------------
  map.addSource('historical-map', {
    type: 'image',
    url: 'https://example.com/scanned-1920s-city.jpg', // public URL, CORS-enabled
    coordinates: [
      [-122.4525, 37.8044], // top-left
      [-122.3890, 37.8044], // top-right
      [-122.3890, 37.7610], // bottom-right
      [-122.4525, 37.7610]  // bottom-left
    ]
  });

  // ---- RASTER LAYER -------------------------------------------------
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'historical-map-layer',
    type: 'raster',
    source: 'historical-map',
    paint: {
      // Start fully opaque; we’ll toggle this later
      'raster-opacity': 0.8
    }
  });

  // ---- OPTIONAL: Insert the layer *under* label layers ----------
  // If you want the map’s labels to stay on top, move the layer
  // before the first symbol layer in the style:
  const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
  if (firstSymbol) {
    map.moveLayer('historical-map-layer', firstSymbol.id);
  }
});
```

### Key points

* **CORS** – The JPG must be served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin:*` (or at least from the same origin) otherwise the browser will block the request.
* **`raster-opacity`** – The paint property that controls opacity. It can be changed at any time with `map.setPaintProperty`.
* **Layer order** – By default the new layer is added on top of everything else. If you want the basemap’s labels to stay visible, move the layer just before the first `symbol` layer (see the snippet above).

---

## 4. UI for toggling opacity

You can expose a simple HTML range slider, a button, or any UI component you like. Below is a minimal example using a range input (0 → 100 % opacity).

```html
<div id="opacity-control" style="position:absolute;top:10px;right:10px;background:#fff;padding:8px;border-radius:4px;">
  <label for="opacitySlider">Historical map opacity</label><br>
  <input type="range" id="opacitySlider" min="0" max="100" value="80">
</div>
```

```javascript
// Hook the slider to the raster-opacity paint property
document.getElementById('opacitySlider').addEventListener('input', (e) => {
  const opacity = Number(e.target.value) / 100; // convert 0-100 → 0-1
  map.setPaintProperty('historical-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
});
```

If you prefer a simple **on/off** button, just toggle between `0` and your desired default (e.g., `0.8`).

```javascript
let visible = true;
document.getElementById('toggleBtn').addEventListener('click', () => {
  visible = !visible;
  map.setPaintProperty('historical-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', visible ? 0.8 : 0);
});
```

---

## 5. When to consider a tiled raster instead of a single image

| Indicator | What to do |
|-----------|------------|
| Image size > 10 MB **or** dimensions > 10 k px | Convert to raster tiles (XYZ, WMTS, or PMTiles) using `gdal2tiles.py`, `maptiler`, or the MapLibre-compatible `pmtiles` CLI. Then add a `raster` source instead of `image`. |
| You need smooth zoom-in beyond the native resolution | Tiling lets the browser request higher-resolution tiles for higher zoom levels, avoiding pixelation. |
| You want to serve the map offline (e.g., kiosk) | Package the tiles into a single PMTiles archive and use the `pmtiles` plugin; the same `raster` source syntax works. |

If you ever hit those limits, the **maplibre-pmtiles-patterns** skill will walk you through the server-less PMTiles workflow.

---

## 6. Full example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Historical Map Overlay</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
    #opacity-control { font-family:sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<div id="opacity-control">
  <label for="opacitySlider">Opacity</label><br>
  <input type="range" id="opacitySlider" min="0" max="100" value="80">
</div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.42, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('style.load', () => {
    map.addSource('historical-map', {
      type: 'image',
      url: 'https://example.com/scanned-1920s-city.jpg',
      coordinates: [
        [-122.4525, 37.8044],
        [-122.3890, 37.8044],
        [-122.3890, 37.7610],
        [-122.4525, 37.7610]
      ]
    });

    map.addLayer({
      id: 'historical-map-layer',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'historical-map',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.8 }
    });

    // Keep labels on top
    const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol');
    if (firstSymbol) map.moveLayer('historical-map-layer', firstSymbol.id);
  });

  // Opacity slider
  document.getElementById('opacitySlider').addEventListener('input', e => {
    const opacity = e.target.value / 100;
    map.setPaintProperty('historical-map-layer', 'raster-opacity', opacity);
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Copy-paste the above into a file, replace the `url` and `coordinates` with your own values, and you have a working overlay with an opacity slider.

---

## 7. Checklist before you go live

| ✅ Item | Why it matters |
|--------|----------------|
| **CORS headers** on the JPG (or host on the same origin) | Prevents “blocked by CORS policy” errors in the browser. |
| **Accurate corner coordinates** (lon/lat, counter-clockwise) | Guarantees the image aligns correctly with the basemap. |
| **Reasonable image size** (< 5 MB for most broadband users) | Keeps initial load time low; larger files should be tiled. |
| **Layer order** – move the raster layer below label layers if you want map labels visible | Prevents your image from obscuring important map text. |
| **Attribution** – add a source attribution entry if required by the map’s license | Keeps you compliant with the scanned map’s copyright. |
| **Responsive UI** – test the opacity control on desktop and mobile | Ensures a smooth user experience across devices. |

---

### TL;DR

1. **Use a `image` source** with the four corner `[lon, lat]` coordinates.  
2. Add a **`raster` layer** that references that source.  
3. Control opacity via the layer’s `raster-opacity` paint property (e.g., a range slider).  
4. Keep the layer below any symbol (label) layers for readability.  
5. If the JPG is huge or you need higher-zoom detail, convert it to raster tiles (XYZ/PMTiles) and use a `raster` source instead.

That’s the “right way” to overlay a scanned historic map on a MapLibre GL JS map while letting users adjust its transparency. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** Yes. Leaflet can’t consume vector tiles directly, but the **MapLibre GL Leaflet** plugin will render any MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap into raster tiles on-the-fly and expose them as a normal Leaflet `TileLayer`.  

Below is a step-by-step guide, the minimal code you need, and the trade-offs you should be aware of.

---

## 1. Why a plugin is required

| Library | Tile types it natively understands |
|---------|--------------------------------------|
| **Leaflet** | Raster tiles only (PNG/JPEG/WebP, WMTS, WMS, etc.) |
| **MapLibre GL JS** | Vector tiles (`vector` source) **and** raster tiles (`raster` source) |
| **MapLibre GL Leaflet** | Bridges the gap – it runs a hidden MapLibre GL instance, draws the style to a canvas, and serves that canvas as raster tiles to Leaflet. |

> **Result:** From Leaflet’s point of view you are still adding a normal raster `TileLayer`.  
> **Limitation:** You lose client-side vector features (no `queryRenderedFeatures`, no dynamic styling per-feature). If you need those interactions you must embed a full MapLibre GL map instead of Leaflet.

---

## 2. Install the plugin

```bash
# Using npm / yarn (recommended for modern builds)
npm install maplibre-gl-leaflet   # or: yarn add maplibre-gl-leaflet

# Or load directly from a CDN in a plain-HTML page
<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<link   href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link   href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link   href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
```

> **Version tip:** Use the latest stable releases of **Leaflet ≥ 1.7**, **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 4**, and **maplibre-gl-leaflet** that match each other. The plugin’s README lists compatible version ranges.

---

## 3. Add a MapLibre-style vector basemap

```html
<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script>
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the Leaflet map (any CRS you like – default EPSG:3857 works)
  const map = L.map('map').setView([40.7128, -74.0060], 12); // New York example

  // 2️⃣ Create a MapLibre-GL-Leaflet layer
  const mlLayer = L.maplibreGL({
    // The URL can be a remote style JSON (MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, etc.)
    // or a local file you host yourself.
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
    // Optional: limit the max zoom that the plugin will request from MapLibre.
    // (Leaflet will still allow higher zooms, but the raster tiles will be up-sampled.)
    maxZoom: 18,
    // Optional: pass any MapLibre GL options you need (e.g., `transformRequest` for auth)
    // transformRequest: (url, resourceType) => {
    //   if (resourceType === 'Source' && url.includes('tiles')) {
    //     return { url: url + '?access_token=YOUR_TOKEN' };
    //   }
    //   return { url };
    // },
  }).addTo(map);

  // 3️⃣ (Optional) Add your own Leaflet overlays on top of the basemap.
  // Example: a GeoJSON point layer with click interaction.
  const myPoints = L.geoJSON(myGeojson, {
    pointToLayer: (feat, latlng) => L.circleMarker(latlng, {radius:6, fillColor:'#ff6600', color:'#fff', weight:1}),
    onEachFeature: (feat, layer) => {
      layer.bindPopup(`ID: ${feat.properties.id}`);
    }
  }).addTo(map);
</script>
```

### What the code does

| Step | What happens under the hood |
|------|-----------------------------|
| **`L.maplibreGL({style})`** | Creates a hidden MapLibre GL map, loads the vector-tile style, and renders it to an off-screen canvas. |
| **`addTo(map)`** | The plugin registers a Leaflet `TileLayer` that, for each requested tile (`z/x/y`), asks the hidden MapLibre map to draw that tile into the canvas and returns the raster image to Leaflet. |
| **`maxZoom`** | Controls the highest vector-tile zoom the hidden MapLibre instance will request. Leaflet can still zoom beyond that, but the raster tiles will be up-scaled (no extra detail). |

---

## 4. Attribution & Tile Usage

* The style you point to (e.g., MapTiler, OpenFreeMap, OpenStreetMap-based providers) usually includes an `attribution` field in its JSON. The plugin automatically adds that to Leaflet’s attribution control.  
* If you host your own vector tiles, make sure the style’s `sources` contain the correct `attribution` strings, or add them manually:

```js
map.attributionControl.addAttribution('© Your Data Provider');
```

---

## 5. When to use this vs. a full MapLibre GL map

| Use case | Recommended approach |
|----------|----------------------|
| **Pure Leaflet UI, existing Leaflet plugins (draw, heatmap, clustering, etc.)** | **MapLibre GL Leaflet** – you keep the Leaflet ecosystem and just swap the basemap. |
| **Need per-feature interaction on the basemap (click a road, query properties, dynamic styling)** | Embed a **stand-alone MapLibre GL map** side-by-side or replace Leaflet entirely. |
| **Offline kiosk / static HTML where you want a single bundle** | Pre-render the vector style to raster tiles (e.g., with `tile-join` or `mbtiles`) and serve them as normal raster tiles – you can still use the plugin, but you could also skip it and use a regular `L.tileLayer`. |
| **Performance-critical mobile app with limited bandwidth** | Vector tiles are still the most efficient; the plugin only adds a thin raster conversion layer, which is negligible compared to the network cost of the vector tiles themselves. |

---

## 6. Gotchas & Tips

| Issue | How to avoid / fix |
|-------|--------------------|
| **CORS errors** when the style or tile URLs are on a different domain. | Ensure the tile server sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain). The plugin forwards the same request headers as MapLibre GL, so any CORS policy that works for a pure MapLibre map will work here. |
| **High-DPI screens** – raster tiles may look blurry if the source style only provides up to `z=14`. | Set `detectRetina: true` on the Leaflet map (default) and make sure the vector source includes higher zoom levels. The plugin will request the appropriate `@2x` tiles automatically. |
| **Tile flicker when panning fast** – the hidden MapLibre map has to render each tile on demand. | Increase the hidden map’s `pixelRatio` (via `L.maplibreGL({pixelRatio: 2})`) or pre-warm the cache by calling `mlLayer.getMap().setZoom()` to a higher zoom once on load. |
| **Styling changes at runtime** – you can call `mlLayer.getMap().setStyle(newStyleUrl)` to swap basemaps without recreating the Leaflet layer. | Keep a reference to the underlying MapLibre map: `const mlMap = mlLayer.getMap();` then `mlMap.setStyle('…')`. |
| **Memory usage** – the hidden MapLibre map holds a full tile cache. | Use the `maxTileCacheSize` option (MapLibre GL) via `mlLayer.getMap().setMaxTileCacheSize(256)` if you run into memory pressure on low-end devices. |

---

## 7. Full minimal example (HTML only)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8"/>
  <title>Leaflet + MapLibre-GL-Leaflet basemap</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.css"/>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css"/>
  <style>#map {height:100vh; width:100%;}</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/leaflet@1.9.4/dist/leaflet.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.3.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl-leaflet@0.0.13/dist/leaflet-maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  const map = L.map('map').setView([51.505, -0.09], 13);

  // MapLibre-GL-Leaflet basemap
  L.maplibreGL({
    style: 'https://api.maptiler.com/maps/streets/style.json?key=YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY',
    // optional: add your own MapLibre GL options here
  }).addTo(map);

  // Example overlay: a simple GeoJSON point
  const geojson = {
    "type":"FeatureCollection",
    "features":[
      {"type":"Feature","properties":{"name":"London Eye"},
       "geometry":{"type":"Point","coordinates":[-0.1195,51.5033]}}
    ]
  };
  L.geoJSON(geojson).addTo(map);
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Replace `YOUR_MAPTILER_KEY` with a valid token (or point to any public style URL that serves vector tiles).

---

## 8. Further reading

* **MapLibre GL Leaflet repo** – installation, API, and advanced options: <https://github.com/maplibre/maplibre-gl-leaflet>
* **MapLibre Style Specification** – what a `style.json` can contain: <https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/>
* **Vector-tile providers** – MapTiler Cloud, OpenFreeMap, OpenMapTiles, etc. (all expose a `style.json` you can plug in directly).
* **If you later decide to drop Leaflet** – see the skill *maplibre-source-wiring* for wiring your own vector-tile source into a pure MapLibre GL style.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Install** `maplibre-gl-leaflet`.
2. **Create** a Leaflet map as usual.
3. **Add** `L.maplibreGL({style: '…'})` as a layer – it turns any MapLibre-style vector-tile basemap into raster tiles that Leaflet can display.
4. **Overlay** your own Leaflet layers (GeoJSON, markers, heatmaps, etc.) on top.

That’s all you need to bring a modern vector-tile basemap into a Leaflet-based application. Happy mapping!
